<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ralpachen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned c. 815–<a href="/years/838">838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Tritsuk Detsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ralpachen, also called Tritsuk Detsen, was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> from about 815 to 838. A lavish patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he promoted the translation of scriptures and the standardisation of terms, and concluded the peace treaty of 821–822 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, recorded on a bilingual pillar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. He was murdered in 838 and succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> remember him as the third of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ralpachen.jpg" alt="Ralpachen" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ralpachen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ralpachen, born about 802, was the second of five brothers and the 40th king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. His elder brother Tsangma took Buddhist vows, while the third, U Dumtsen, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a>, is described in the sources as unfit to reign. Under Ralpachen the Tibetan empire reached its largest extent, taking in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, Khotan, Balti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> and Hunza, Zhangzhung, almost all of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, and the area of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>. His power rested on the military leadership of Zhang 'Bro sTag, who raided to within two days' journey of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> capital in 816 and attacked the Chinese town of Yanzhou in 819.</p><p>Fighting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Uyghur Khaganate ended with a Sino-Tibetan treaty completed in 821 or 822, which kept the peace for more than two decades; Buddhists in both countries are said to have sought mediation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> also made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and apparently with Nanzhao in 822. A bilingual account of the treaty and the border, inscribed on a stone pillar erected in 823, stands outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, and similar pillars were set up in China and at the border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ralpachen was a generous supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. He invited craftsmen, scholars and translators from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and Khotan, and ordered that translations be made directly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. Translation was aided by the Mahāvyutpatti, a Sanskrit-Tibetan lexicon giving standard Tibetan equivalents for thousands of terms. He built a nine-story temple at 'U shang, assigned revenue from seven families to support each monk, and introduced standard weights and measures based on Chinese ones. His nickname means the man with long braids, from the story that he let monks sit on their ends.</p><p>According to the most common Tibetan tradition he was murdered by two pro-Bon ministers, who set his anti-Buddhist brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a> on the throne. Other accounts say he slipped on the steps of the temple of Maldro, or, following the Old Book of Tang, fell ill and died, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> manuscript holds a prayer for the king's health. He died late in 838 and was buried near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-valley">Yarlung Valley</a>. Langdarma then nearly destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Tibetans remember Ralpachen as the third of the three Dharma Kings, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ralpachen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Tibetans remembered Songtsen Gampo, Trisong Detsen and <b>Ralpachen</b> as the three Dharma kings, and the memory of the empire underpinned the legitimacy of later Tibetan rulers and lineages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>An Indian version was translated into Tibetan in the reign of King <b>Ralpachen</b> (r. 815 to 838), and it most closely matches the Chinese version of Jñānagupta and Dharmagupta and the Nepalese Sanskrit version.</blockquote><a href="/work/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Lhasa, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.14&amp;year=829" aria-label="Lhasa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6922 1081 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ralpachen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7372 1351h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7372 1351h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.14&amp;year=829">What was here in 829 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
